IN CICD how many stages are there ?
In a typical CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery) pipeline, there are several stages that the code goes through before it is deployed to production. The exact number and configuration of stages can vary depending on the organization and specific requirements, but here are some of the common stages:
Code development: Developers write and test their code on their local machines or in a development environment.
Version control: The code is then committed to a version control system, such as Git, which allows multiple developers to collaborate on the same codebase.
Continuous Integration (CI): The code is automatically built and tested every time a developer makes changes and commits them to the version control system. The CI stage ensures that the code can be built and that all unit tests pass.
Artifact creation: The compiled code is packaged into an artifact, such as a Docker container or a JAR file.
Continuous Delivery (CD): The artifact is deployed to a staging environment, where it undergoes further testing and validation.
User acceptance testing (UAT): The application is tested by users or stakeholders to ensure that it meets the business requirements.
Production deployment: Once the artifact has been thoroughly tested and validated, it is deployed to production.

In which language we create pipelines ?
In Jenkins, you can create pipelines using the Jenkins Pipeline plugin. The Jenkins Pipeline plugin supports defining pipelines using either a Declarative Pipeline syntax or a Scripted Pipeline syntax.
The Declarative Pipeline syntax is a more structured and opinionated way of defining pipelines, designed to make it easier to get started with Jenkins Pipelines. Declarative Pipelines are defined using a simple, human-readable syntax that is based on YAML, and it allows you to define the various stages, steps, and other elements of your pipeline.
The Scripted Pipeline syntax is a more flexible and powerful way of defining pipelines. Scripted Pipelines are defined using Groovy, a popular scripting language for the Java platform. Groovy provides a lot of flexibility in how you define your pipelines, allowing you to do things like define functions and use loops and conditionals.
Both Declarative and Scripted Pipeline syntaxes are supported by Jenkins, and the choice of which one to use depends on the specific requirements of your project and the preferences of your team.

what all types of pipelines can be created ?
In Jenkins, there are two main types of pipelines that can be created:
Freestyle Jobs: These are the traditional jobs that you can create in Jenkins. They are highly configurable, and you can add multiple build steps, triggers, and post-build actions to a single job.
Jenkins Pipeline: Pipeline is a powerful, flexible, and extensible way to define continuous delivery pipelines. With Pipeline, you can define your entire build pipeline in code, including build, test, and deployment stages. You can also define the stages that make up your pipeline, such as build, test, and deploy, and you can define the conditions that trigger each stage.
Within Jenkins Pipeline, there are two different syntaxes for defining pipelines:
Scripted Pipeline: This is the traditional, Groovy-based way to define a pipeline in Jenkins. It is highly flexible and provides full programmatic control over the build process, but can be more complex to set up and maintain.
Declarative Pipeline: This is a newer, YAML-based way to define a pipeline in Jenkins. It is designed to be simpler and easier to read than the Scripted Pipeline syntax, while still providing a high degree of flexibility and extensibility.
Overall, Jenkins Pipeline is a powerful and flexible tool that allows you to define your entire build, test, and deployment process in code, and there are a variety of ways to define pipelines depending on your needs and preferences.

Sonarqube is used for ?
SonarQube is a popular open-source tool used for continuous code quality inspection and management. It is primarily used for performing static code analysis, which is a technique for examining source code to detect bugs, vulnerabilities, and other code smells.
SonarQube can analyze a wide range of programming languages, including Java, C#, JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Ruby, and others. It checks the code against a set of predefined rules, best practices, and quality standards and provides a comprehensive report highlighting the issues found in the code, along with suggestions for how to fix them.
Some of the key benefits of using SonarQube include:
Improved code quality: SonarQube helps identify and eliminate bugs, vulnerabilities, and other code smells, improving the overall quality of the codebase.
Enhanced developer productivity: By providing quick feedback on code quality issues, SonarQube can help developers catch issues early in the development process and reduce the time spent on bug fixing.
Consistent code quality: By enforcing a set of predefined rules and quality standards, SonarQube helps ensure that the codebase meets a consistent level of quality across all developers and teams.
Better team collaboration: SonarQube provides detailed reports and metrics that can be shared across teams, facilitating collaboration and knowledge sharing.
Overall, SonarQube is a powerful tool for improving code quality and reducing technical debt, making it an essential tool in any software development workflow.

IN pipeline if any of stage failed then can user move to next stage ?
In a pipeline, the behavior of subsequent stages after a failure in a previous stage depends on how the pipeline is configured.
By default, if a stage fails in a pipeline, the pipeline will immediately stop, and the remaining stages will not be executed. This behavior is designed to prevent the deployment of faulty code or artifacts into production environments.
However, it is possible to configure the pipeline to continue executing subsequent stages even if a previous stage has failed. This can be useful in cases where a failure in one stage does not necessarily mean that the entire pipeline should be stopped.
To configure a pipeline to continue after a stage failure, you can use the "ignoreError" option in the stage definition. This will allow the pipeline to continue executing even if the stage fails, and you can add additional logic to handle the error and take appropriate action.
Overall, whether or not a pipeline can continue after a stage failure depends on the pipeline configuration, and it is up to the pipeline designer to determine the appropriate behavior for their specific use case.
For example, the following pipeline stage definition will continue executing the pipeline even if the build stage fails:

stage('Build') {
    steps {
        // build steps here
    }
    post {
        always {
            echo 'Build stage completed'
        }
        failure {
            echo 'Build failed, but pipeline will continue'
            // additional error handling logic here
            ignoreError true
        }
    }
}

This code block represents a Jenkins pipeline stage definition for the "Build" stage.
The stage is defined with the name "Build" using the stage() method. Inside the stage, there is a steps block that contains the actual build steps that will be executed. These build steps can include compiling code, running tests, generating artifacts, or any other necessary tasks to build the project.
After the steps block, there is a post block that defines actions to be taken after the stage has completed. In this case, there are two actions defined:
An always block that will be executed regardless of whether the stage succeeds or fails. This block simply prints a message to the console indicating that the stage has completed.
A failure block that will be executed only if the stage fails. This block prints a message indicating that the build failed, but also sets the ignoreError option to true, which allows the pipeline to continue executing even if this stage fails.
The ignoreError option is a pipeline option that can be set on any stage, and it allows the pipeline to continue executing even if the stage fails. This can be useful in situations where a failure in one stage does not necessarily mean that the entire pipeline should be stopped.
Overall, this code block represents a simple example of a Jenkins pipeline stage definition, with additional logic to handle failures and allow the pipeline to continue executing even if the build stage fails.

In pipeline if we want to ignore failure of 1 build and want to move to other job how to do ?
To ignore the failure of one build and continue to execute other jobs in the pipeline, you can use the "try-catch" block in Jenkins pipeline syntax. The "try-catch" block allows you to execute a block of code and catch any exceptions that are thrown during execution. You can use this block to catch the exception thrown when the build fails, and then continue to execute the other jobs in the pipeline.
Here's an example of how to use the "try-catch" block to ignore the failure of one build and continue to execute other jobs:

pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                sh 'make'
            }
        }
        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                try {
                    sh 'deploy'
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    echo 'Ignoring deploy failure...'
                }
            }
        }
        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                sh 'test'
            }
        }
    }
}

In this example, the pipeline has three stages: "Build", "Deploy", and "Test". The "Build" stage compiles the code using the "make" command. The "Deploy" stage deploys the build to a test environment using the "deploy" command, but is wrapped in a "try-catch" block to catch any exceptions thrown during deployment. If the "deploy" command fails, the exception is caught, and the message "Ignoring deploy failure..." is printed to the console. The "Test" stage runs the automated tests using the "test" command, regardless of whether the "Deploy" stage succeeded or failed.
By wrapping the "Deploy" stage in a "try-catch" block, you can ignore the failure of that stage and continue executing the pipeline. However, it's important to note that ignoring build failures can lead to the deployment of potentially faulty code into production environments. As such, it's generally recommended to use caution when ignoring build failures and to only do so when it's safe to continue executing the pipeline without fixing the build failure.

How to run parallel stages in pipelines ?
To run parallel stages in a Jenkins pipeline, you can use the "parallel" block in pipeline syntax. The "parallel" block allows you to define multiple stages that can be executed in parallel, as long as they don't have dependencies on each other.
Here's an example of how to use the "parallel" block to run two stages in parallel:

pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Parallel stages') {
            parallel {
                stage('Build') {
                    steps {
                        sh 'make'
                    }
                }
                stage('Test') {
                    steps {
                        sh 'test'
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

In this example, the pipeline has one stage called "Parallel stages", which contains a "parallel" block with two stages: "Build" and "Test". Each stage contains a single step that executes a shell command using the "sh" step. When the pipeline is executed, the "Build" and "Test" stages will be executed in parallel.
You can add as many stages as you like to the "parallel" block, as long as they don't have dependencies on each other. If you need to include stages that do have dependencies, you can use the "dependsOn" keyword to specify the dependencies between stages.
It's important to note that running stages in parallel can increase the overall speed of the pipeline, but it can also increase the load on the Jenkins server. If you have a large number of parallel stages, you may need to consider the resources available on your Jenkins server and adjust your pipeline accordingly.

Tools used to monitor cicd platform ?
There are several tools that can be used to monitor a CI/CD platform:
Jenkins Monitoring Plugin: Jenkins has a monitoring plugin that provides real-time monitoring of Jenkins master and slave nodes. The plugin monitors system statistics such as CPU usage, memory usage, disk I/O, and network I/O.
Prometheus: Prometheus is an open-source monitoring system that can be used to monitor a CI/CD platform. Prometheus collects metrics from different sources, including Jenkins, and provides a time-series database to store the collected data. It also has a built-in dashboard for visualizing the data.
Grafana: Grafana is an open-source dashboarding tool that can be used to visualize the data collected by Prometheus or other monitoring tools. Grafana has pre-built dashboards for various monitoring tools, including Jenkins, and it can be used to create custom dashboards to monitor specific aspects of the CI/CD platform.
ELK Stack: The ELK stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) is a popular open-source toolset used for log management and monitoring. It can be used to monitor logs from Jenkins and other components of the CI/CD platform. Elasticsearch is used to store and search logs, Logstash is used to collect, process, and forward logs, and Kibana is used to visualize and analyze the logs.
Nagios: Nagios is a popular open-source tool for monitoring IT infrastructure. It can be used to monitor various aspects of a CI/CD platform, including server and application availability, resource usage, and performance. Nagios can send alerts when it detects problems, and it can be integrated with other tools like Slack, PagerDuty, and email to notify teams about problems.
These are just a few examples of the tools that can be used to monitor a CI/CD platform. The choice of tool(s) will depend on the specific needs and requirements of the organization.

at the end of each pipeline which artifacts are generated ?
At the end of a pipeline, one or more artifacts may be generated depending on the specific pipeline and its purpose. Artifacts are files or packages that are created during the build and deployment process, and they can include things like executable binaries, installers, documentation, and configuration files.
Some common artifacts that are generated at the end of a pipeline include:
Deployable packages: These are packages that are created to be deployed to a production environment. Examples include Docker images, WAR files, or ZIP files containing the necessary files and configurations for an application.
Test results: Test results are often generated as artifacts and can include test reports, code coverage reports, and other metrics generated during the testing process.
Documentation: Documentation is often generated as an artifact, including user manuals, API documentation, and release notes.
Build artifacts: These are the files generated during the build process, such as compiled code, libraries, and other dependencies.
Configuration files: Configuration files are often generated as artifacts and can include environment-specific configurations for applications and services.
The specific artifacts that are generated at the end of a pipeline will depend on the pipeline's purpose and the needs of the organization.

